\description{
Build the UGF of a public forest \strong{from the cadastral parcels}, each UGF
carrying the number of its ONF forest parcel. Rule (Pascal, 2026-10-08): forest
parcels are only a partition of cadastral parcels; UGF are always obtained by
grouping and cutting \strong{cadastral} parcels, the cadastre is never warped,
and it is the ONF layer, which overflows, that is adjusted.

By default this function starts from the forest and \strong{finds}
the cadastral parcels that belong to it (spec 058):
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Candidates}: cadastral parcels (PCI, IGN) touching the union of
the ONF parcels, restricted to the commune \code{insee}.
\item \strong{Calage}: the ONF layer is rubber-sheeted onto the candidates'
boundaries (\code{\link{caler_onf_sur_cadastre}}).
\item \strong{Selection}: a candidate belongs to the forest when its owner is a
\strong{public person} according to the DGFiP legal-entity file
(\code{\link{load_parcelles_personnes_morales}}) \strong{and} the warped ONF
covers at least \code{seuil_couverture} of it. Neither condition is enough
alone: at Couchey (21), three communal parcels of 22 to 57 ha are covered at
2–3 \% (moorland outside the \emph{régime forestier}), and a 0.04 ha private
parcel is covered at 56 \% by an ONF overflow.
\item \strong{Cutting by snapping}: within each selected parcel, the pieces cut
by the ONF parcels are reduced to their core by a morphological opening of
radius \code{tol / 2} (\code{larg_hors / 2} for the uncovered remainder, whose
cores under \code{ha_hors} are dropped). The strips left over join the core
they share the longest boundary with; detached parts under
\code{min_part_m2} join their longest-boundary neighbour. An ONF limit closer
than \code{tol} to a cadastral limit thus \strong{snaps} onto it: a parcel is
cut only where the ONF limit really departs from it.
\item \strong{Final attachments}, over all tenements (neighbours share at least
1 m of boundary, measured within 10 cm): an ONF piece under \code{seuil} ha
whose neighbours all belong to one other UGF, and which does not touch its
own, moves to that UGF (up to 5 passes); an uncovered piece (\code{cad~})
under \code{seuil_hors} ha joins its longest-boundary neighbour; a whole UGF
under \code{seuil} ha is shared out among its longest-boundary neighbours.
}

The cutting works on a centimetre grid; each parcel is finally re-tiled on its
\strong{original} geometry, so the cadastre does not move even by a
millimetre.

Measured at Couchey (21200): 63 candidates, 19 parcels kept (493.79 ha), 63
UGF, none uncovered, 85 tenements, smallest UGF 2.11 ha, in about 15 s once
the sources are loaded.
}
\section{Approximation of the régime forestier}{

Neither the PCI nor the Etalab cadastre records whether a parcel is under the
\emph{régime forestier}. The authoritative source is the prefectoral order of
application, which is not published parcel by parcel. The "public owner × ONF
coverage" test is an \strong{approximation} of it, to be checked against the
management plan.
}

